\section{Introduction}
The Gnome project has evolved significantly over the last decade, particularly with the introduction of \texttt{gnome-shell}. While the original API was clearly designed for extensibility, it has grown increasingly complex due to the diverse implementations available across GNU/Linux distributions. This fragmentation increases maintenance costs, introduces interoperability issues, and complicates the transition to newer versions of systems.

\subsection{Background}
The Gnome API was designed to facilitate access to Gnome-specific functionalities. Historically, it relied heavily on the \texttt{gobject} interface, which required careful handling of object references. As newer applications migrated to the \texttt{libgnome} library, the core API remained largely unchanged, making extension difficult.

\subsection{Problem Statement}
The core problem lies in the lack of a unified, standardized approach to accessing Gnome functionality. Developers face challenges in:
\begin{itemize}
\item Ensuring consistent behavior across different distributions.
\item Supporting legacy applications that rely on older API versions.
\item Maintaining backward compatibility as APIs change.
\end{itemize}

